            <Placemark id="1990">
                <name>Waldorf Astoria New York </name>
                <description>
                category  : Sleep_Splurge &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 301 Park Ave  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : https://waldorfastoria3.hilton.com/en/hotels/new-york/waldorf-astoria-new-york-NYCWAWA/index.html  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Another famous luxury hotel that is among the more notable in New York, with a long history in a lavish Art Deco building that has housed many a celebrity. Perhaps one of the most famous guests was President Franklin Roosevelt, and a secret platform that is believed to have been used by Roosevelt to travel directly from the train to his hotel room so he could hide his paralysis from the public survives under the hotel. The traincar that Roosevelt is believed to have used is parked on the platform. Sadly, neither the secret platform nor the traincar are open to public. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#106</styleUrl>
                <Point id="1989">
                    <coordinates>-73.97384,40.75671,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="1992">
                <name>Westgate New York City </name>
                <description>
                category  : Sleep_Splurge &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 304 East 42nd St  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : https://www.westgateresorts.com/hotels/new-york/midtown-manhattan/westgate-new-york-city/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : The former Hilton, an iconic landmark, neo-gothic hotel, only a few blocks from Times Square and Grand Central Station. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#106</styleUrl>
                <Point id="1991">
                    <coordinates>-73.971961,40.749419,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="1994">
                <name>On foot </name>
                <description>
                category  : Get around &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Central Park is a haven for people looking to walk, hike, stroll, or jog. The entire park is friendly for hiking and strolling, with many hidden corners and a variety of terrains to explore. Speed walkers and joggers have three ideal spots in the park: the Park Drives, which circle the park and have a special lane for joggers; the Reservoir, which has a very well-used trail surrounding it; and three separate bridle paths, one adjacent to the Reservoir, one at the North Meadow, and one at the southern side of the park, all of which are loops that extend about 1–1.5 miles. The Central Park Conservancy operates guided walking tours year-round ($25 adult); check the [https://www.centralparknyc.org/official-central-park-tours website] for details. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#170</styleUrl>
                <Point id="1993">
                    <coordinates>-93.6583579,50.0529974,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="1996">
                <name>Arsenal </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 64th Street and Fifth Avenue  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A picturesque brick building that actually predates the park. It was built in 1851 to serve as a munitions supply depot for the New York State National Guard, and was designed to look like a medieval fortress, with battlements overlooking the area. Today the building holds a refreshment stand and WPA murals depicting park activities. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="1995">
                    <coordinates>-73.9713,40.76763,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="1998">
                <name>Balto Statue </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : East Drive at 67th Street  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A popular monument to the famous sled dog who successfully led a sled team through a treacherous blizzard to deliver medicine to [[Nome (Alaska) &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="1997">
                    <coordinates>-73.97106,40.76996,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2000">
                <name>Bethesda Terrace and Fountain </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Terrace Drive (72nd St.)  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : One of Manhattan's favorite meeting points, the centerpiece of this Terrace is the '''Angel of the Waters''' fountain, dedicated in 1873 and an enduring icon of the park. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="1999">
                    <coordinates>-73.97092,40.77406,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2002">
                <name>Central Park Zoo </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 5th Avenue and 64th Street  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.centralparkzoo.com/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Small and gem-like, New York's &quot;oldest, newest zoo&quot; opened in its current guise in 1988, although animals in various zoo incarnations have resided here since the 1860s. This zoo is fairly small and doesn't have as many large animals as you might expect, but this zoo does include sea lions, penguins, polar bears, monkeys, red pandas, and exotic birds in pleasant exhibits. Next door is a children's zoo, covered in the cost of admission, which has a barnyard animals you can pet, a duck pond, and lots of play areas for kids. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2001">
                    <coordinates>-73.97197,40.7679,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2004">
                <name>Dairy </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 65th St  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Built in the 1870s as a dairy farm, with a structure designed to resemble a country church. Today it is a visitor center and gift shop for the park, housed in a beautiful structure. Next door is the '''Chess &amp; Checkers House''', another visitor center and the volunteer headquarters, with a number of chess and checkers tables under a shady pergola. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2003">
                    <coordinates>-73.9737,40.7691,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2006">
                <name>Friedsman Carousel </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 65th St  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A vintage carousel built in 1908 and situated on this spot since the 1950s, it's the fourth carousel to inhabit this location and is beautifully painted. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2005">
                    <coordinates>-73.97524,40.76993,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2008">
                <name>Grand Army Plaza </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Fifth Ave  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A public square at the southeast corner of the park which marks one of the primary entrances to Central Park. The square is named for the Union Army of the Civil War and sports a gilded bronze statue of Union General William Sherman and the Pulitzer Fountain, which is crowned with a bronze figure of Pomona, the Roman goddess of fruit. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2007">
                    <coordinates>-73.97332,40.76443,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2010">
                <name>The Pond </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Central Park South  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Near Grand Army Plaza, the Pond offers a tranquil setting just within the boundaries of the park. A rustic wood structure, &quot;Cop Cot,&quot; overlooks the pond from an outcrop near the Sixth Avenue entrance. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2009">
                    <coordinates>-73.97407,40.76583,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2012">
                <name>Sheep Meadow </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : west side of the park  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A spacious green lawn that was originally home to a herd of sheep, which grazed in the meadow and tended to in their nearby pen - a Victorian style building which today is the '''Tavern on the Green''' restaurant (see [[#Eat &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2011">
                    <coordinates>-73.97502,40.77185,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2014">
                <name>Strawberry Fields </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_South End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Central Park West at 72nd Street  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.centralparknyc.org/visit/things-to-see/south-end/strawberry-fields.html  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : So named in 1981 in memory of John Lennon, the former Beatle, who was murdered close by outside his home in the Dakota building. Lennon's widow Yoko Ono, who still lives in the Dakota, subsequently donated $1 million to upgrade the area with hundreds of tree and flower species, including strawberries. The area serves as a Garden of Peace and includes a memorial floor mosaic (donated by the [[Italy &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2013">
                    <coordinates>-73.974608333333,40.775411111111,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2016">
                <name>Belvedere Castle </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Great Lawn &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 79th St  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.centralparknyc.org/visit/things-to-see/great-lawn/belvedere-castle.html  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Sitting on Vista Rock, one of the highest points in the park, the castle provides excellent views of Central Park, particularly to the north. It is a popular spot for photography and contains a visitor center and a nature conservatory. Just below the castle to the north is '''Turtle Pond''', a small, swamp-like pond holding various fish, frogs, insects, and birds. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2015">
                    <coordinates>-73.9691,40.7794,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2018">
                <name>Conservatory Pond </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Great Lawn &lt;br&gt;
                address  : east side of the park  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Most well known as the Model Boat Pond, visitors can often see a racing regatta between members of the Model Yacht Club, or rent a model boat from a boathouse and cafe on the pond. Just to the north of the pond is the '''Alice in Wonderland''' sculpture depicting the Tea Party scene, and on the west side of the pond is the '''Hans Christian Andersen''' sculpture, which shows the writer seated on a bench reading a book to his ''Ugly Duckling'' character. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2017">
                    <coordinates>-73.96713,40.77422,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2020">
                <name>Great Lawn </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Great Lawn &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : At the center of Central Park, the Great Lawn is a large clearing with lawns and ballfields, perfect for ballgames, sunbathing, and picnicking. Just to the east of the Lawn is the '''Obelisk''', a 71-foot tall structure which is the oldest man-made object in the park, having been erected in Heliopolis, Egypt, around 1500 BC. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2019">
                    <coordinates>-73.96648,40.78137,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2022">
                <name>The Lake </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Great Lawn &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : The lake is a fine setting for a serene afternoon in the park. Rental boats are available from the '''Loeb Boathouse''' (on the eastern side of the lake) for a ride on the water. The '''Bow Bridge''', a Central Park landmark, spans the middle of the lake. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2021">
                    <coordinates>-73.97252,40.77574,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2024">
                <name>The Ramble </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Great Lawn &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 79th St  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A sort of mini forest, described by its designer as a &quot;wild garden,&quot; the Ramble is sculpted out of a wooded hillside, with winding paths, rocky outcrops, secluded glades, and a tumbling stream. If one travels through the ramble when the trees are full, it is easy to lose sight of the city's skyscrapers; it's as if you're no longer in Manhattan. The Ramble is also an excellent place to bird watch, with over 250 species of birds that stop here on their migration. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2023">
                    <coordinates>-73.96907,40.77763,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2026">
                <name>The Reservoir </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_The Reservoir &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Constructed between 1858 and 1862, the Reservoir is a vast urban lake that covers 106 acres of Central Park and is the largest body of water within Manhattan. Renamed the &quot;Jacqueline Kennedy Onassis Reservoir&quot; in 1994, the Reservoir is probably best known for the 1.58 mile track that runs around its edge and which is a favorite for joggers, who can both run and enjoy the spectacular views of the city skyline. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2025">
                    <coordinates>-73.9619,40.7862,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2028">
                <name>Charles A. Dana Discovery Center </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_North End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 110th St  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : On the north shore of the Harlem Meer, the Discovery Center holds education and community programs and seasonal exhibits, as well as offering a popular place for catch-and-release fishing. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2027">
                    <coordinates>-73.95144,40.79706,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2030">
                <name>Conservatory Garden </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_North End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 5th Ave  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A six-acre garden which is Central Park's only formal garden, representing Italian, French, and English landscape styles, with formal plantings and numerous sculptures. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2029">
                    <coordinates>-73.95282,40.79392,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2032">
                <name>Great Hill </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_North End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : west side of the park  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : One of the highest points in the park, the Great Hill is a hilltop meadow surrounded by stately elm trees and serves as an excellent place to picnic. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2031">
                    <coordinates>-73.9589,40.79666,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2034">
                <name>Harlem Meer </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_North End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : east side of the park  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : This 11 acre lake is one of Central Park's finest spots; surrounded by flowering trees and inhabited by several fish and turtle species. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2033">
                    <coordinates>-73.95125,40.79637,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2036">
                <name>North Woods </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_North End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : west side to middle of the park, north of 101st St  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.centralparknyc.org/things-to-see-and-do/attractions/north-woods.html  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : None of Central Park is actual virgin forest, but this is the closest any part of the park gets to feeling like genuine forest. It is beautiful and cool in hot weather. However, like a real forest, it is a mosquito habitat, so make sure to use insect repellent before walking through this part of the park. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2035">
                    <coordinates>-73.95577,40.79771,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2038">
                <name>The Pool </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_North End &lt;br&gt;
                address  : west side of the park  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : One of the most idyllic and tranquil landscapes in Central Park, the Pool is an excellent spot for quiet contemplation, with its grassy banks and nearby waterfalls. A stream, '''The Loch''' flows northeast from the Pool, through a stream valley called '''The Ravine'''. A trail that follows the Loch, winding under a canopy of tall trees as the stream goes over several waterfalls and passes under a couple of stone arches. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2037">
                    <coordinates>-73.96055,40.79472,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
