            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2064">
                <name>The Boathouse </name>
                <description>
                category  : Eat &lt;br&gt;
                address  : on Park Drive North on The Lake  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.thecentralparkboathouse.com/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Held in the Loeb Boathouse on the Lake, the Boathouse includes a sit-down restaurant with tables overlooking the lake, a sit-down bar area outside, and an express cafe with counter service for quick takeout and eat-in food. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#78</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2063">
                    <coordinates>-73.96877,40.77519,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2066">
                <name>Le Pain Quotidien </name>
                <description>
                category  : Eat &lt;br&gt;
                address  : in the Mineral Springs Pavilion at the northern end of the Sheep Meadow  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.lepainquotidien.com/store/central-park-mineral-springs/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A branch of this local chain of sit-down restaurants with organic options like handmade bread and vegan ice cream. Pre-packaged picnic boxes are also available. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#78</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2065">
                    <coordinates>-73.97437,40.77302,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2068">
                <name>Tavern on the Green </name>
                <description>
                category  : Eat &lt;br&gt;
                address  : 67th St &amp; Central Pk W  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://tavernonthegreen.com/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : This iconic restaurant has appeared in dozens of films as one of New York's most famous dining spots. The historic building once served as a sheep paddock before being converted to a restaurant in 1934, soon becoming a favorite destination for New Yorkers' special occasions. Although the setting is as lovely as it's always been, the restaurant itself has developed a reputation for providing overpriced food that isn't all that good. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#78</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2067">
                    <coordinates>-73.97774,40.77219,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
